## Install

Add the package to your project.

<PackageInstall name="@neoworks-dev/sdk" />

## Create the client

The client takes the OAuth client id of your app and the scopes it asks for. The scopes that name a collection, such as `@neoworks/calendar:read`, tell the SDK to keep an encrypted local store and sync it. The examples on this page use the calendar collection, so they ask for its read and write scopes.

```ts
import Neoworks from '@neoworks-dev/sdk'

export const neoworks = new Neoworks({
  client: 'my-calendar-app',
  name: 'My Calendar',
  scopes: [
    'openid',
    'profile',
    'email',
    '@neoworks/calendar:read',
    '@neoworks/calendar:write',
  ],
})
```

The `client` value is the id your app is registered with on the Neoworks auth server. `name` is the name the user sees on the consent screen.

<CodeAside {...signInExample}>

## Sign in

Restore the session of an earlier visit, and start the sign-in if there is none.

`signin()` sends the browser to the Neoworks authorization page. After the user approves, Neoworks sends the browser back to `/callback` on the origin of your app. That page only needs to finish the sign-in:

```ts
await neoworks.signin()
```

The call exchanges the code for tokens, accepts the grant for the collections and starts the first sync. It resolves to the signed-in `User`.

</CodeAside>

## Create and read an item

<MarginNote>The first write is slower than the ones after it: it loads the encrypted local store and the wasm core.</MarginNote>

The calendar schema is generated as the module `@neoworks-dev/schemas/calendar`. It exports one resource per model, named in the plural, and the enum `CategoryColor`. A calendar is a container, and an event is an item under a calendar.

```ts
import calendarSchema, { CategoryColor } from '@neoworks-dev/schemas/calendar'

const { calendars, events } = neoworks.collection(calendarSchema)

const work = await calendars.create({
  name: 'Work',
  color: CategoryColor.blue,
  isDefault: false,
})

const standup = await events.create({
  calendar: work.id,
  uid: crypto.randomUUID(),
  title: 'Standup',
  start: new Date('2026-10-14T09:00:00Z'),
  end: new Date('2026-10-14T09:15:00Z'),
  showWithoutTime: false,
  sequence: 0,
  deleted: false,
  createdAt: new Date(),
  updatedAt: new Date(),
})

const stored = await events.get(standup.id)
console.log(stored.title, stored._meta.calendar)
```

The item carries the parent container as `calendar`, the same name the schema gives the container model. Fields of the schema sit at the top level of the object, and the SDK adds `_meta` with the container id and the state of the node.

